  <h2>Therapy in English and Farsi</h2>
  <p>Rozy is a Farsi-speaking therapist, and she works in either language, or in the mix of both that a lot of bilingual families actually speak at home. If you have ever tried to describe grief, shame, or a family obligation in your second language, you know that something goes missing in the translation. It is tiring, and it puts a layer of glass between you and the person listening.</p>
  <p>She also brings particular experience with Persian and Middle Eastern clients, and more broadly with people from multicultural and immigrant backgrounds. That matters beyond vocabulary. It means not having to explain why a decision that looks straightforward from the outside carries the weight of a whole family, or why honoring your parents and living your own life can feel like a choice you are not willing to make.</p>
  <p>Prefer to keep sessions in English? That is completely fine, and common. The point is that the option is there, and that the cultural context does not need translating either way.</p>
{% endcall %}
